Figma

Contextual Moat: Score 5 / 5 
Workflow Depth: Figma is deeply embedded across product teams as the single system of record for design systems, prototype flows, developer handoffs (Dev Mode), and product strategy (FigJam).

Switching Costs: Extremely high. Migrating an enterprise away from Figma requires porting entire design system libraries, breaking cross-functional handoff links, retraining non-design stakeholders (PMs, engineers), and suffering massive productivity loss.

Data Advantage: Score 3 / 5
Proprietary Signal: Figma possesses an extensive corpus of vector manipulation data, component usage patterns, layout decisions, and cross-functional review interactions.

Compounding Limits: While their component graphs and design system telemetry provide proprietary context for native AI tools, raw design layout data does not create an insurmountable, self-reinforcing AI flywheel compared to pure data-network products.

Platform Exposure: Score 4 / 5
Encroachment Risk: Low risk of foundational platform owners (OpenAI, Apple, Google) eating Figma’s core wedge. Simple text-to-UI prompts generated by base LLMs lack the granular canvas control, design-system alignment, multiplayer state management, and production-ready code generation that product teams require.

Structural Defense: Even if platform giants ship free native design/wireframing features inside OS environments or chat interfaces, Figma operates as a complete multiplayer workflow layer rather than a single standalone UI feature.

